{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788213149,"nonce":"XJzfDxT0fukjS4FcFjQjBU5l5J8=","message":"ACCEPTANCE RUN — orchestrator, BORGES bench, 2026-08-31. Driven by hand against\n`accept/4087644-order-honesty.md` (BORGES has no python, so the `.py` could not\nbe used). **Phases 1–4 PASS. Phase 5.1b FAILS.** Issue stays OPEN.\n\nThe core claim of this issue is fixed and demonstrated. Two defects found that\nthe fix did not cover, one inside this issue's own contract.\n\nPHASE 1 — already-doing-it. PASS, all six checks.\n\n    wear {pawn:1026, thing:41948}          -\u003e accepted 1, action.journal_seq 17\n    wear {pawn:1026, thing:41948} (again)  -\u003e accepted 0\n                                              gate    \"already-doing-it\"\n                                              queue   false\n                                              reason  \"already running an equivalent job, so\n                                                       Pawn_JobTracker.TryTakeOrderedJob would have\n                                                       returned true without taking the order\n                                                       (Job.JobIsSameAs against curJob)\"\n                                              action.journal_seq 18   \u003c- the amended contract\n    journal {types:[\"action\"]} seq 18      -\u003e verdict.by_gate {\"already-doing-it\": 1}\n    wear {... queue:true}                  -\u003e same gate, queue TRUE, and a SHARPER reason:\n                                              \"...enqueued NOTHING — the order was not queued and\n                                               left no trace, because its JobIsSameAs early-out\n                                               fires before requestQueueing is ever read\"\n    draft + move-to own cell               -\u003e gate \"already-there\" (distinct gate, untouched)\n\n1.3 is the important one and it holds: the wasted order still journals, and\n`verdict.by_gate` makes \"which of my instructions were redundant\" answerable\nfrom the aggregate rather than only per-call. The reason strings name the game's\nown member, not a phrase of ours.\n\nPHASE 2 — attribution. PASS, with one SCRIPT ERROR (not a code error).\n\nAll six keys present on `pawn {sections:[\"state\"]}`: `job_id`, `player_forced`,\n`job_giver`, `work_giver`, `job_start_tick`, `ordered`. A think-tree job read\n`job_giver \"JobGiver_WanderColony\"`, `ordered false` — 2.6's point, demonstrated.\n\n**2.2b CANNOT PASS AS WRITTEN, and the code is right.** The table expects\n`ordered == true` after `wear {pawn:B, thing:AP}`. Measured:\n\n    job_giver \"ThinkNode_QueuedJob\"   player_forced true\n    work_giver null                   ordered FALSE\n\n`PawnActs.cs` computes `ordered = queuedNode \u0026\u0026 workGiver != null \u0026\u0026 forced` —\nexactly the triple this document's own prose defines two paragraphs below the\ntable. **A `wear` order has no WorkGiver, so `ordered` is false by construction.**\nThe script contradicts itself; the code matches its stated definition. Amend\n2.2b rather than the code.\n\nWorth flagging for 4.1, which is being authored right now against this surface:\nthe FIELD NAME invites the same misreading the script made. A wear order *was*\nordered by the player and `ordered` says false. Its honest meaning is \"this is a\nprioritized-WORK order\", not \"the player caused this\". A checklist item that\nreads `ordered` to answer \"did my order take?\" will be wrong for every direct\norder.\n\nPHASE 3 — the job queue. PASS, including the sharpest check.\n\n    wear A (running) + wear B {queue:true}  -\u003e queue_total 0 -\u003e 1\n                                              queued row: own job_def \"Wear\", own job_id 1097,\n                                              job_start_tick NULL (not the -1 sentinel)\n    wear A {queue:true} (collides)          -\u003e accepted 0, gate \"already-doing-it\",\n                                              queue flag true, queue_total UNCHANGED at 1\n\n3.3 is the one that matters and it is exactly right: the collision enqueues\nnothing, the queue does not grow, and only the gate reports it.\n\nPHASE 4 — forced apparel. PASS, and the demonstration is stronger than asked.\n\nAfter the two queued wears completed under `advance {ticks:4000}`:\n\n    forced_count 2\n    Apparel_BasicShirt  forced false     \u003c- her own policy-worn starting clothes\n    Apparel_Pants       forced false\n    Apparel_Parka       forced TRUE      \u003c- force-worn\n    Apparel_Tuque       forced TRUE      \u003c- the QUEUED one, which also re-proves phase 3\n\nPolicy-worn and force-worn distinguishable on ONE pawn, which is what 4.4 asks.\nZero red errors throughout, so 4.6's `OutfitForcedHandler.IsForced` hazard was\nsuccessfully avoided by the `ForcedApparel`-plus-Contains route.\n\nPHASE 5 — 5.2 and 5.3 PASS. **5.1b FAILS.**\n\n    work-priorities {manual:true} then {set:[…]}  -\u003e ok, counts.unit \"matrix cells\",\n                                                     action.journal_seq 39\n    journal {types:[\"red_error\"]}                 -\u003e 0\n\nThe e8f2c32 regression guard holds: the matrix path still journals despite\n`Accepted.Count` being 0 there.\n\n**THE FAILURE.** `undraft` then `tend {pawn:38946, target:38949}`:\n\n    accepted 0, gate \"drafted-only\"   \u003c- 5.1a passes\n    action.journal_seq 0              \u003c- 5.1b requires \u003e= 1\n\nAnd it is not a stamping quirk — the row does not exist. Grepped the session's\njournal file directly: **zero `\"verb\":\"tend\"` rows.** Meanwhile refused `wear`,\n`move-to` (both `already-there` and `drafted-only`), `draft` and `undraft` all\njournalled correctly, so this is not the contract failing generally.\n\nCAUSE, located rather than inferred. `Source/AutoRimmer/PawnEmergencyVerbs.cs`,\n`tend`'s gate-refusal path:\n\n    if (!self \u0026\u0026 !ProviderGate(doctor, drafted: true, …, out string gate, out string reason))\n    {\n        outcome.No(doctor, gate, reason);\n        return outcome.Result(V, 0, new Dictionary\u003cstring, object\u003e { … });   // \u003c- hardcoded 0\n    }\n\nThe rejection is recorded in the RESULT and then the call returns a hardcoded\n`0` for the journal seq, bypassing `ActOn` entirely. `tend`'s other exits go\nthrough `TendRow(...)` -\u003e `ActOn` and journal correctly; only the early gate\nrefusal does not. **The same hardcoded-0 early return appears at two more sites\nin that file** (`extinguish` and `beat-fire`), so the emergency verbs as a group\nneed checking — these are the M1 emergency set, and a refused emergency order is\nprecisely the one you most want in the ledger.\n\n(The `extinguish` `if (!burning)` early return is arguably different — nothing\nwas refused because nothing was attempted — but `tend`'s is an unambiguous\nrefusal with a gate and a reason, which is exactly what comment #1 says must\njournal.)\n\nSEPARATE DEFECT FOUND, OUT OF THIS ISSUE'S SCOPE — filed on its own.\n\n`move-to {queue:true}` **silently ignores the queue flag.** Measured: pawn\nrunning a Goto to [120,120]; `move-to {to:[90,120], queue:true}` returned\n`accepted: 1`; the queue stayed empty; and after 150 ticks the pawn was at\n[95,107] — x FALLING, i.e. walking to [90,120]. It replaced the running job and\nreported success.\n\nCause: `PawnOrderVerbs.cs` `move-to` calls\n`FloatMenuOptionProvider_DraftedMove.PawnGotoAction(to, p, dest)` directly and\nnever reaches `TryTakeOrderedJob`, so there is no `requestQueueing` to pass — and\nit never reads a `queue` argument at all. `wear {queue:true}` on the same bench\nqueues correctly, so the argument is honoured by one verb and silently dropped by\nits sibling. That is the same family as this issue (a job verb reporting success\nfor an order that did not happen) but a different mechanism, so it is its own\nissue rather than a comment here.\n\nVERDICT\n\nThe claim under test — `TryTakeOrderedJob`'s `JobIsSameAs` early-out making\n`accepted` a lie — is fixed, demonstrated live, and the journal aggregate that\nmakes redundant orders visible works. **Not closing:** 5.1b is a real failure of\nthis issue's own amended contract, in the emergency verbs, and 4.1 is being\nauthored against this surface right now.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DEMONSTRATE\n\n- Phase 2.2's `job_giver == \"ThinkNode_QueuedJob\"` NOTE-vs-FAIL degradation on a\n  null jobGiver; the field was populated on every read here.\n- `equip` and the remaining emergency verbs (`repair`, `man-turret`,\n  `rest-until-healed`, `fire-at-will`) — only `tend` was driven.\n- The `attack` probe, which the script itself excludes with reasons.\n- 3.4's check 5.12c, which the script says now contradicts the amended contract\n  and must be edited from `None` to `\u003e= 1`. **Not edited** — flagging it here so\n  it is not forgotten; it is a one-line change in `accept/3.4-pawn-orders.py:905`\n  and its `.md` twin.","files":null}]}